=== start ===
# checkpoint: start
The canteen has been empty since dawn. You shake it by your ear anyway, and nothing moves inside. # place: The rim
You have walked since the stars were out. The cap is too hot to hold for long. Your shirt is stiff with salt where the sweat dried, and wet again under the arms. Your lips are split at the corners.
The rock you have been climbing all morning ends at an edge. Below it lies a basin, so wide that the far side is a paler line in the haze. # page
To the north a thin line might be a road. Past it, something in the haze might be a town.
At the foot of the slope a water tower stands over a few roofs. There's no smoke and nothing moves. A tower means a well, or it used to.
You go down sideways and dig your heels in. Stones slide out from under your boots and clatter away below you. # page
Halfway down you stop sweating. You know what that means, and you keep going. If you sit down now, you might not get up.
Up close the tank is rusted brown and stands on thin iron legs. A gun mount is bolted to the rail at the top, with nothing in it. Somebody kept watch from up there. # page # place: The farm
The house has lost its roof. The fence posts lean. The goat pen holds dry droppings and no goats.
A rope lies coiled beside the well, knotted at even lengths along its whole run. Every knot is dry.
The tap under the tower squeals when you work the handle. The pipe coughs a thread of rust onto your boots and gives nothing else. You hold it open a while longer anyway.
Behind the tower, what's left of a shed has folded in on itself. Under the fallen roof sheet, in the shade, sits a pickup. # page
It's old and square, with a flat grille and round lamps. The paint has gone to grey primer. The tires are soft but whole, and the bed is boxed in with welded plate.
A machine gun sits on a swivel behind the cab, its barrel wrapped in rag. A pair of steel pins hold the ring. One pin is bright and new, the other worn dull. A small welder's mark is cut into the ring.
Someone has scratched a ladle into the driver's door, deep, with a knife point.
The hinge shrieks when you pull the door, and something small scrabbles out from under the bed. The cab smells of oil, rope and old tobacco. A key stands in the ignition, with a brass tag on its ring stamped with a number worn half smooth. # page
You twist the key. The starter grinds, slows and quits.
You twist it again. The engine catches for a breath, shakes the whole cab and dies. You call the truck something you wouldn't call a dog.
You lean your forehead on the hot wheel and wait. Then you try a third time. It coughs, misses, coughs again, and holds. The idle is rough and lopsided, and the mirror shakes with it. It's the best sound you have heard in a long time.
You ease out of the shed. The roof sheet scrapes the length of the cab and slides off the tailgate, and the basin opens up in front of the hood. # page
The fuel needle sits just above empty. The town in the haze had better be close.
-> END
